{"name":"Ogen Melon","hex":"#d7b235","fetched":"2026-10-16","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"melon","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Some of the plants of the family Cucurbitaceae grown for food, generally not including the cucumber.","The large, round to ovoid fruits that have rinds and are of such plants."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a light pinkish orange color, like that of melon flesh."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/melon"}],"entries":[{"title":"Melon","blocks":[{"text":"A melon is any of various plants of the family Cucurbitaceae with sweet, edible, and fleshy fruit. It can also specifically refer to Cucumis melo, commonly known as the \"true melon\" or simply \"melon\". The term \"melon\" can apply to both the plant and its fruit. Botanically, a melon is a kind of berry, specifically a \"pepo\". The word melon derives from Latin melopepo, which is the latinization of the Greek μηλοπέπων (mēlopepōn), meaning \"melon\", itself a compound of μῆλον (mēlon), \"apple\", treefruit \" and πέπων (pepōn), amongst others \"a kind of gourd or melon\". Many different cultivars have been produced, particularly of the true melon, such as the cantaloupe and honeydew."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e7/Cantaloupe_and_canary_melon.jpg/330px-Cantaloupe_and_canary_melon.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Melon","wikidata":"Q5881191"},{"title":"Shades of orange","blocks":[{"text":"In optics, orange has a wavelength between approximately 585 and 620 nm and a hue of 30° in HSV color space. In the RGB color space it is a secondary color numerically halfway between gamma-compressed red and yellow, as can be seen in the RGB color wheel. The complementary color of orange is azure. Orange pigments are largely in the ochre or cadmium families, and absorb mostly blue light."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3b/Shades_of_Orange.svg/330px-Shades_of_Orange.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shades_of_orange","wikidata":"Q19847194"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e7/Cantaloupe_and_canary_melon.jpg/500px-Cantaloupe_and_canary_melon.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e7/Cantaloupe_and_canary_melon.jpg/1280px-Cantaloupe_and_canary_melon.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e7/Cantaloupe_and_canary_melon.jpg/500px-Cantaloupe_and_canary_melon.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Canary melon and cantaloupe","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACantaloupe_and_canary_melon.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8e/Watermelon_and_melon_in_India.jpg/500px-Watermelon_and_melon_in_India.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8e/Watermelon_and_melon_in_India.jpg/1280px-Watermelon_and_melon_in_India.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8e/Watermelon_and_melon_in_India.jpg/500px-Watermelon_and_melon_in_India.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Watermelon and melon in India","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AWatermelon_and_melon_in_India.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1e/Cucumis_metuliferus_fruit_-_whole_and_cross_section.jpg/500px-Cucumis_metuliferus_fruit_-_whole_and_cross_section.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/